WEEK–2: Single-Node, Single-Broker Kafka Cluster
Aim

To set up a single-node, single-broker Kafka cluster and perform basic Kafka operations such as:

Creating a topic
Describing a topic
Producing messages
Consuming messages
Saving consumed messages into a file
PART 1 — Start Docker

Open Terminal 1.

Step 1: Check Docker
docker --version

You should get something like:

Docker version ...

If Docker is not running:

sudo systemctl start docker
PART 2 — Download Kafka and ZooKeeper Images

According to the PDF, use the following Docker images.

Step 2: Download ZooKeeper
docker pull wurstmeister/zookeeper

Wait until the download completes.

Step 3: Download Kafka
docker pull wurstmeister/kafka

Now both required images are available.

PART 3 — Run ZooKeeper
Step 4: Start ZooKeeper
docker run -d --name zookeeper -p 2181:2181 wurstmeister/zookeeper
What this does
docker run → creates and runs a container
-d → runs in background
--name zookeeper → gives container the name zookeeper
-p 2181:2181 → maps ZooKeeper port
wurstmeister/zookeeper → ZooKeeper image

ZooKeeper uses port 2181.

PART 4 — Run Kafka Broker
Step 5: Start Kafka

Run:

docker run -d --name kafka -p 9092:9092 --link zookeeper:zookeeper \
-e KAFKA_ZOOKEEPER_CONNECT=zookeeper:2181 \
-e KAFKA_ADVERTISED_LISTENERS=PLAINTEXT://localhost:9092 \
-e KAFKA_LISTENERS=PLAINTEXT://0.0.0.0:9092 \
wurstmeister/kafka
Important

If the command appears on multiple lines, enter it exactly as shown.

Kafka uses:

9092

as its port.

PART 5 — Check Kafka and ZooKeeper
Step 6: Check running containers
docker ps

You should see something similar to:

zookeeper
kafka

Both should be running.

Step 7: Check Kafka logs
docker logs kafka

If Kafka has started successfully, you should see Kafka startup-related messages.

PART 6 — Generate Kafka Cluster ID

The PDF then gives the Kafka storage commands.

First enter the Kafka container:

docker exec -it kafka bash

Now you are inside the Kafka container.

Step 8: Generate Cluster ID
KAFKA_CLUSTER_ID="$(bin/kafka-storage.sh random-uuid)"

This generates a random Kafka cluster ID and stores it in:

KAFKA_CLUSTER_ID
PART 7 — Format Kafka Storage
Step 9: Format the Kafka log directories
bin/kafka-storage.sh format --standalone -t $KAFKA_CLUSTER_ID -c config/server.properties

This formats the Kafka storage using the generated cluster ID.

PART 8 — Start Kafka Server
Step 10: Start Kafka server
bin/kafka-server-start.sh config/server.properties

Kafka server will start.

Note: This command normally keeps the terminal occupied while Kafka is running. If you need another terminal for the next operations, open Terminal 2.

The PDF also includes an Apache Kafka 4.0.0 Docker option:

docker pull apache/kafka:4.0.0

and:

docker run -p 9092:9092 apache/kafka:4.0.0

It also mentions the native image:

apache/kafka-native:4.0.0

For your lab, don't mix this with the Wurstmeister setup unless your faculty specifically asks for Kafka 4.0.0. The PDF contains both sections.

PART 9 — Create Kafka Topic

Now create the topic mentioned in the PDF.

The topic is:

quickstart-events
Step 11: Create topic
bin/kafka-topics.sh --create --topic quickstart-events --bootstrap-server localhost:9092

Expected output is similar to:

Created topic quickstart-events.

This creates a Kafka topic called quickstart-events.

PART 10 — Describe the Topic
Step 12: Describe topic
bin/kafka-topics.sh --describe --topic quickstart-events --bootstrap-server localhost:9092

You may get output similar to:

Topic: quickstart-events
PartitionCount: 1
ReplicationFactor: 1
Partition: 0
Leader: 0
Replicas: 0
Isr: 0
Meaning
Term	Meaning
Topic	Name of the topic
PartitionCount	Number of partitions
ReplicationFactor	Number of copies
Leader	Broker handling the partition
Replicas	Brokers containing the partition
ISR	In-Sync Replicas

Because this is a single-node/single-broker setup, the replication factor is 1.

PART 11 — Start Kafka Producer

The producer is used to send messages to Kafka.

Step 13: Start producer
bin/kafka-console-producer.sh --topic quickstart-events --bootstrap-server localhost:9092

After running this command, type messages.

For example:

This is my first event

Press Enter.

Then:

This is my second event

Press Enter.

These messages are sent to:

quickstart-events

PART 12 — Start Kafka Consumer

Now open Terminal 2.

Enter the Kafka container if required:

docker exec -it kafka bash
Step 14: Start consumer
bin/kafka-console-consumer.sh --topic quickstart-events --from-beginning --bootstrap-server localhost:9092

The consumer reads the messages from the topic.

You should see:

This is my first event
This is my second event

The important option is:

--from-beginning

It tells Kafka to read the available messages from the beginning.

PART 13 — Stop Consumer

When you have verified the messages, press:

Ctrl + C

This stops the consumer.

PART 14 — Kafka Connect Section

Your PDF also contains a Kafka Connect section.

Kafka Connect is used to connect Kafka with external systems such as files and databases.

Step 15: Configure plugin path

Inside the Kafka directory, run:

echo "plugin.path=libs/connect-file-4.0.0.jar" >> config/connect-standalone.properties

This adds the file connector plugin path to:

connect-standalone.properties

PART 15 — Create test.txt
Step 16: Create a test file
echo -e "foo\nbar" > test.txt

This creates:

test.txt

with:

foo
bar
PART 16 — Work With test-topic

The PDF then demonstrates another topic:

test-topic
Step 17: Start producer
bin/kafka-console-producer.sh --broker-list localhost:9092 --topic test-topic

Notice: Here we are using the PDF's exact --broker-list command.

Type:

Hello

Press Enter.

Then:

Kafka Test Message

Press Enter.

PART 17 — Consume and Save Messages to File
Step 18: Start consumer
bin/kafka-console-consumer.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
--topic test-topic --from-beginning > test.sink.txt

This consumes the messages and redirects the output into:

test.sink.txt

So instead of simply displaying the messages on screen, they are saved in a file.

PART 18 — Display the Saved File
Step 19: Use cat
cat test.sink.txt

Expected output:

Hello
Kafka Test Message

This confirms that the Kafka messages were successfully consumed and stored in the file.

PART 19 — Optional tee Method

The PDF also gives an alternative using tee.

bin/kafka-console-consumer.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
--topic test-topic --from-beginning | tee test.sink.txt
Difference

>:

> test.sink.txt

saves the output to the file.

tee:

| tee test.sink.txt

displays the output on the screen and also saves it to the file.
